Z||000006||cobra427||05-09-2000||12:45 AM||||GT, Heres a thought. Some of the new Alternators do not like to charge a low battery.If your battery is not fully charged at the installation of your alternator, you can kill the new alternator real quick. You said that you had a new battery but did you check to see if it was fully charged. Don't assume that it was just because it is new. It may have had enough "juice" to start the car and not be fully charged.<BR>Just another idea.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Racer 1||05-09-2000||01:54 AM||||Check your fuseable links also right by the battery. Sometimes if those fuseable links get corrorded it wont charge good. how many amp alternator do you have? I bought the 90 some amp one at autozone. Works real good.||||reg||3
Z||000008||Frank R||05-09-2000||02:01 AM||||Alternators should be called amplifiers.  These units will not make any voltage alone.  They must be stimulated by an outside source, ie the battery.  If a battery's voltage is less than 12.0 volts, the altenator will not have an output voltage sufficient to charge a battery.  The output of most altenators is 13.2-13.6 volts.  Only a dc generator will charge a completely discharged battery.  We don't use them in modern cars, as the mfg costs are too high and they have a poor ampacity output.  In order to properly check the out put of any alternator, the battery must be fully charged.  Alternators will amplify after the pulley speed exceeds 2500 rpm.  These units need 12 volts excitation voltage and about 1/2 amp, then with correct pulley speed, the unit will produce up to 100 amps at 13.3 volts.       Frank R...||||reg||6
Z||000009||GT Bastard||05-09-2000||03:34 AM||||The problem ended up being a low battery.  I had it charged fully and now she's holding her charge perfectly.  One of the connections on the alternator had fallen off and allowed the battery to get extremely low. After I fixed it, I had expected the alternator to charge it back up.  Well, it didn't.  Thanks for the advice everyone. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||05-09-2000||10:36 AM||||"I need full power, Scotty!"<BR>"I canna do it, Cap'n!"<BR>Of course, he does it anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||05-09-2000||07:46 PM||||Frank, huh? I've run a number of alternator powered cars with no battery or a battery well below 12V. All most cars need is enough juice to fire the coils to start. <P>Most U.S. made alternators cary enough residual magnetism to self energize. All they need is a tiny bit to spin up.<P>Some really recent ones may crab, and maybe some imports, but every GM Ford or Chrysler I've ever jumped started charged unless the alternator or regulator was actually bad. I'm talking battery so dead the head lights don't glow on alot of them. (Batteries often won't last long after you do that kind of deep drain. Expect to replace it in a few days to a couple months.)<P>If it's dropping off like that take the alternator to a diferant store and test it.<P>Also don't forget to carefully check the thin wire from the battery positive. That wire runs the car and charges the battery. The main lead only goes to the starter. (This is all Fiero but 84. 84 had the fuse links on the starter.)<P>You should get around 13VDC off a fully charged battery. Once the car starts the voltage should rise to 14-15VDC. If it doesn't the car isn't charging. You have either a wiring problem or a dead alternator.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-09-2000).]||||reg||1
